5
00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.420
And now we're doing the Ultimate Office rewatch podcast just for you.

6
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:19.400
Each week, we will break down an episode of The Office and give exclusive behind-the-scenes stories that only two people who were there can tell you.

7
00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:20.540
We're The Office ladies.

8
00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:23.740
Hello!

9
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:25.020
Hi there.

10
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:27.980
Today we're going to talk about body language.

11
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.360
Let's do it.

12
00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:34.780
It's Season 6, Episode 23, written by Justin Spitzer and directed by Mindy Kaling.

13
00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:36.320
Here's a summary.

14
00:00:37.300 --> 00:00:45.080
Michael tries to interpret the body language of Jim and Pam's potential client, who he believes is interested in him.

15
00:00:45.660 --> 00:00:49.940
That client is Donna, the manager of Sid and Dexter's.

16
00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:51.600
She's come in to buy some printers.

17
00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:58.920
Meanwhile, Gabe is recruiting for Saber's Print in All Colors Minority Executive Program.

18
00:00:59.620 --> 00:01:01.140
But Dwight's going to get involved.

19
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:02.540
He has opinions.

20
00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:03.080
Yeah.

21
00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.480
Fast fact number one, this episode was directed by our very own Mindy Kaling.

22
00:01:10.380 --> 00:01:19.760
This is the first episode of The Office that Mindy has directed, but she had also directed the Subtle Sexuality and the Third Floor Office web series.

23
00:01:20.020 --> 00:01:23.480
And those were each three miniature episodes long.

24
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:23.900
Mm-hmm.

25
00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:28.440
She will go on to direct Michael's Last Dundies in Season 7.

26
00:01:29.120 --> 00:01:30.980
And something I realized.

27
00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:31.700
What?

28
00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.480
I was thinking about it.

29
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:39.380
Season 6 was a really big season for our cast and crew members to direct episodes.

30
00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.240
Yes, and also some of our writer's assistants to get episodes to write.

31
00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:48.360
Well, you know, next week, Rainn Wilson is going to direct his first episode, the cover-up.

32
00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.660
So, I needed a stat.

33
00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:51.560
Oh.

34
00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:52.460
I was inspired.

35
00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.380
Is this like a Dr. Thibodeau moment?

36
00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:55.220
A little bit.

37
00:01:55.320 --> 00:01:56.100
Okay, let's hear it.

38
00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:56.980
Inspired by.

39
00:01:57.980 --> 00:02:06.020
Of the 26 episodes in Season 6, only eight episodes were directed by a non-cast or crew member.

40
00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:10.400
And we only had three new directors come on the show.

41
00:02:10.720 --> 00:02:14.420
They were Reggie Hudlin, Seth Gordon, and Mark Webb.

42
00:02:15.220 --> 00:02:17.560
Everybody else was returning or cast or crew.

43
00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.140
Only three new for such a big season.

44
00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:20.760
I know.

45
00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:22.960
Well, I love a good stat.

46
00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:24.140
Thank you, Angela.

47
00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:24.680
You're welcome.

48
00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:27.720
Do you love a good mini deep dive?

49
00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:28.840
Do I?

50
00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:35.400
My fast fact number two is a mini deep dive, but I would like to hear the sting that my son made.

51
00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:36.540
It's an oldie but a goodie.

52
00:02:36.680 --> 00:02:37.360
Oh, I love it.

53
00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:38.360
The deep dive sting.

54
00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:40.640
You got a deep, you got a dive.

55
00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:43.060
You put them together, get a deep dive.

56
00:02:44.660 --> 00:02:45.520
OG sting.

57
00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:47.440
I know, one of our very firSt.

58
00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:53.460
All right, lady, this is a deep dive into the subject of body language.

59
00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:57.900
Anthropologist Ray Birdwistel.

60
00:02:58.560 --> 00:02:59.560
Birdwistel.

61
00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:01.880
Birdwistel.

62
00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:03.640
Birdwistel.

63
00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:04.440
Birdwistel.

64
00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:06.440
That looks like Birdwistel.

65
00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:16.220
Well, Ray pioneered the original study of body language or nonverbal communication, which he called kinesics.

66
00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:24.420
He determined that the average person actually speaks for only 10 or 11 minutes a day.

67
00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:26.980
He does not hang out with us.

68
00:03:27.080 --> 00:03:28.840
He's never met us.

69
00:03:29.140 --> 00:03:29.740
What?

70
00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:30.880
I know.

71
00:03:31.100 --> 00:03:32.160
Who are these people?

72
00:03:32.380 --> 00:03:33.100
I don't know.

73
00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:37.860
But he said that the average sentence only takes about two and a half seconds to say.

74
00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:43.060
Again, I don't know who Ray is hanging out with.

75
00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:43.940
Ray Whistleblower.

76
00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:45.160
Wait, it's not Whistleblower.

77
00:03:45.820 --> 00:03:46.380
Birdwistel.

78
00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:47.000
Oh, my God.

79
00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:48.900
Ray Birdwistel.

80
00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:55.560
He also believed that over 65% of our communication is done nonverbally.

81
00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:11.260
When it comes to business, which applies to this episode, in analysis of thousands of recorded sales interviews and negotiations during the 1970s and 1980s,

82
00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:21.720
researchers found that in business encounters, body language accounted for between 60 to 80% of the impact made around a negotiating table.

83
00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:26.800
So, like, if you have, like, confident body energy, I'm fascinated.

84
00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:27.560
Keep going.

85
00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:28.100
Okay.

86
00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:36.040
They also found that people form up to 80% of their initial opinions about a person in less than four minutes.

87
00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:37.600
Wow.

88
00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:39.140
You have four minutes, guys.

89
00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:40.340
You have four minutes.

90
00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:48.540
Studies also showed that when people are negotiating over the telephone, the person with the stronger argument usually wins.

91
00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:59.460
But this is not true when negotiating face-to-face because, overall, many final decisions were based on what we see rather than what we hear.

92
00:05:00.420 --> 00:05:00.980
Hmm.

93
00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:01.460
Hmm.

94
00:05:02.140 --> 00:05:03.300
Get ready for this, though.

95
00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:03.900
Okay.

96
00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:11.420
So, you know, a big part of this episode is everyone in the office kind of telling Michael that Donna does not have feelings for him.

97
00:05:11.540 --> 00:05:14.120
Or some people think she might.

98
00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.780
There's sort of a debate happening between Pam and the rest of the bullpen.

99
00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:20.300
But it's only Pam and the rest of the bullpen.

100
00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:20.560
Yeah.

101
00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:23.820
But I feel like Pam represents people that might think that.

102
00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:24.880
Who?

103
00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:26.140
I don't know.

104
00:05:26.200 --> 00:05:29.460
When I watched it, I kept asking the same questions Pam was asking.

105
00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:31.600
Like, why is she still here?

106
00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.660
Yes, as an audience member.

107
00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:33.900
Yes.

108
00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:38.860
But within the office, the only voice we hear that there might be interest is Pam's, right?

109
00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:39.200
Yeah.

110
00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:48.140
Well, from the book, the definitive book of body language by Alan and Barbara Peace from September 2006,

111
00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:54.960
most women have the brain organization to out-communicate any man on the planet.

112
00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:55.920
That's a quote.

113
00:05:56.760 --> 00:05:57.920
I'm just saying.

114
00:05:58.020 --> 00:05:58.820
You're just quoting.

115
00:05:59.160 --> 00:05:59.920
I'm quoting.

116
00:06:01.040 --> 00:06:11.020
Magnetic resonance imaging brain scans clearly show why women have a far greater capacity for communicating with and evaluating people than men do.

117
00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:21.940
It is because women have between 14 and 16 areas of the brain to evaluate others' behavior versus men's four to six areas.

118
00:06:22.440 --> 00:06:24.020
That is fascinating.

119
00:06:24.580 --> 00:06:26.480
Literally more areas of the brain.

120
00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:36.900
This book says this explains how a woman can attend a dinner party and rapidly work out the state of the relationships of the other couples at the party, who's had an argument,

121
00:06:37.080 --> 00:06:39.940
who likes who, who doesn't like who, and so on.

122
00:06:40.160 --> 00:06:47.900
And it explains why, from a woman's standpoint, men don't seem to talk much, and from a man's standpoint, women seem to never shut up.

123
00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:49.920
That's a quote from the book.

124
00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:55.480
It is so funny to me because there will be some parent thing that we attend.

125
00:06:55.660 --> 00:07:00.140
We go to so many, you know, for school and stuff, soccer, and we'll leave.

126
00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:02.320
And I always call it the car ride home conversation.

127
00:07:02.320 --> 00:07:05.360
Like, I can't wait for the car ride home conversation.

128
00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:05.760
Yes.

129
00:07:05.860 --> 00:07:08.560
That's where we get to download about all the stuff we saw at the event.

130
00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:13.020
And I get in the car, and I'm like, Josh, did you catch that?

131
00:07:13.180 --> 00:07:13.920
And he's like, what?

132
00:07:14.040 --> 00:07:14.700
What are you talking about?

133
00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:15.480
I'm like, ah!

134
00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:16.380
Yeah.

135
00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:17.800
Same.

136
00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:18.460
Yeah.

137
00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:19.720
Car ride home.

138
00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:20.180
Ugh.

139
00:07:20.920 --> 00:07:23.960
Lee likes the car ride home because I tell him all the stuff I saw.

140
00:07:24.400 --> 00:07:26.520
I have even more information about this.

141
00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:27.460
I couldn't stop.

142
00:07:28.460 --> 00:07:35.480
Research by psychologists at Harvard University showed how women are far more alert to body language than men.

143
00:07:36.100 --> 00:07:41.960
They showed men and women short films with the sound turned off of a man and a woman communicating,

144
00:07:42.200 --> 00:07:47.440
and the participants had to decode what was happening just by reading the expressions on the screen.

145
00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:59.040
The research showed that women were able to read the situation correctly 87% of the time, but men could only do it 42% of the time.

146
00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:01.320
Oh, sorry, guys.

147
00:08:02.140 --> 00:08:29.020
Oh, sorry, guys.

148
00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:34.700
And so they were very finely attuned to these signs.

149
00:08:34.700 --> 00:08:35.160
Yeah.

150
00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:44.880
I think this is proof why Pam, a new mother and a woman, is reading the body language correctly and no one else is.

151
00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:47.820
Do you think Justin Spitzer had that in mind?

152
00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:49.300
I doubt it.

153
00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:50.760
But it makes sense.

154
00:08:50.940 --> 00:08:53.540
It is backed up by scientific research.

155
00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:55.500
I loved all of that.

156
00:08:55.660 --> 00:08:56.120
Did you like that?

157
00:08:56.260 --> 00:08:57.060
I could hear more.

158
00:08:57.380 --> 00:08:59.200
Okay, I feel like I've been talking a long time.

159
00:08:59.720 --> 00:09:05.100
Fast fact number three, Angela is all yours because Angela got in touch with Hide.

160
00:09:05.480 --> 00:09:07.220
Yes, we have been trading messages.

161
00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:09.580
We finally connected, and I am so excited.

162
00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:15.180
Well, you know, he had that great talking head and happy hour, and then he comes back again in this episode.

163
00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:15.960
I know.

164
00:09:15.960 --> 00:09:22.980
So Hide Toshiomura plays Hide in the warehouse, and I asked him about his time on the show.

165
00:09:23.480 --> 00:09:25.220
He had the most wonderful answers.

166
00:09:25.460 --> 00:09:30.360
I'm going to sprinkle them throughout the episode, but I thought I would start with two of the questions I asked him in Fast Fact 3.

167
00:09:30.700 --> 00:09:30.940
Great.

168
00:09:31.260 --> 00:09:34.200
My first question to Hide was our standard question, Jenna.

169
00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:35.960
How did you come to be on the office?

170
00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:37.260
Here's what he said.

171
00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:44.140
Way before doing that big talking head, you know, in happy hour, he did background work as an extra for the show.

172
00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:51.100
At that time, he said he had no idea what the show was about, but his manager called him and asked if he wanted to do some work as an extra.

173
00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:53.620
So he said, sure, and went along with it.

174
00:09:54.320 --> 00:09:58.120
After some time of being a background player, his manager called him and said,

175
00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:04.740
they want you to audition for an unknown bigger role in the show, like a featured role.

176
00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:10.940
He said the role wasn't actually defined at the time, so he had no idea what they wanted or what to expect.

177
00:10:10.940 --> 00:10:11.460
Hmm.

178
00:10:12.020 --> 00:10:17.160
He went to the audition, and he said there was a wide variety of different people from all backgrounds.

179
00:10:17.600 --> 00:10:22.380
He said that when it was time for his audition, he thought he completely botched it.

180
00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:23.060
Oh, no.

181
00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:23.560
Yeah.

182
00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:32.000
He said he was embarrassed, and he went to his car, and he got in his car, and as he was about to turn it on, he decided, no, I can't let it end like this.

183
00:10:32.620 --> 00:10:33.060
What?

184
00:10:33.500 --> 00:10:33.900
Yeah.

185
00:10:34.140 --> 00:10:38.500
He got out of his car and immediately asked for a re-audition on the spot.

186
00:10:38.600 --> 00:10:39.400
I love this.

187
00:10:39.400 --> 00:10:45.220
Then he said, they were like, sure, and he auditioned again, and he said, I bombed again.

188
00:10:45.420 --> 00:10:46.100
Oh, no.

189
00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:56.060
He said he felt defeated, and he went back home, and when he was at his lowest and about to go to bed, he got a call from his manager saying, you got the job.

190
00:10:56.380 --> 00:10:57.100
Oh, my gosh.

191
00:10:57.100 --> 00:10:59.400
This is every actor's story, I have to say.

192
00:10:59.660 --> 00:10:59.710
Yes.

193
00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:01.320
How many times has this happened to you?

194
00:11:01.380 --> 00:11:01.860
So many.

195
00:11:01.960 --> 00:11:04.440
You're like, oh, that was horrible, and then they're like, oh, guess what?

196
00:11:04.480 --> 00:11:04.920
You got it.

197
00:11:04.920 --> 00:11:08.520
Or you're like, I nailed it, and they're like, we've gone another way.

198
00:11:09.020 --> 00:11:09.500
Yes.

199
00:11:09.920 --> 00:11:11.040
So many times.

200
00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:21.540
Well, I remember when they did this audition process, they wanted to fill out the warehouse, and they knew now that the warehouse was going to be more than just background,

201
00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:23.880
that they were going to want to throw lines to people.

202
00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:29.540
So they were trying to fill the warehouse with people who could do speaking roles as well.

203
00:11:29.720 --> 00:11:31.240
So I totally remember this.

204
00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:38.280
Well, I also asked Hede if he had any favorite memories from his time on the set, and this is what he shared, and Jenna, it's so sweet.

205
00:11:38.420 --> 00:11:38.700
What?

206
00:11:39.340 --> 00:11:48.940
Here's what he said, and I quote, I finally remember a moment when I had to do ad-libbing on the spot with Jenna for one of the scenes, and even though my English is not very good,

207
00:11:48.940 --> 00:11:53.200
I had a fun time trying my best to keep the conversation going and entertaining.

208
00:11:53.500 --> 00:11:57.320
At that moment, I remembered why I love acting so much.

209
00:11:57.520 --> 00:12:00.480
Oh, that means so much to me.

210
00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:01.180
I know.

211
00:12:01.580 --> 00:12:02.860
Well, I remember that.

212
00:12:02.860 --> 00:12:06.440
It was in season nine, and he was great.

213
00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:08.960
I just loved connecting with him.

214
00:12:09.080 --> 00:12:11.560
I'm going to share a few more of his stories along the way.

215
00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:21.620
But, lady, before we get into the episode, would you like to know what we were doing the week we filmed this episode in March of 2010?

216
00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:24.000
Is this a bit of Angela's Digital Clutter?

217
00:12:24.180 --> 00:12:25.140
It sure is.

218
00:12:27.560 --> 00:12:28.940
Angela's Digital Clutter.

219
00:12:30.360 --> 00:12:31.200
Thank you.

220
00:12:32.440 --> 00:12:38.320
Apparently, Jenna, I was very into making banana bread during this time.

221
00:12:38.320 --> 00:12:42.820
And I was bragging to you in an email about my banana bread.

222
00:12:43.240 --> 00:12:45.300
And I had brought some to set.

223
00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:51.260
And you had tried it, and you really liked it, and you emailed me asking me for my banana bread recipe.

224
00:12:52.160 --> 00:12:55.940
To which I replied, four to five mashed bananas.

225
00:12:56.180 --> 00:12:57.680
Use ripe ones.

226
00:12:58.140 --> 00:12:59.520
Why did I like it?

227
00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:01.080
Like, you know what?

228
00:13:01.280 --> 00:13:04.040
A novice banana bread baker wouldn't know.

229
00:13:04.280 --> 00:13:04.540
Okay.

230
00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:10.420
Then I said, two cups all-purpose flour, one cup sugar, or less if you can't see your toes.

231
00:13:10.800 --> 00:13:12.240
I'm making jokes here?

232
00:13:12.380 --> 00:13:13.920
I'm making jokes in my recipe.

233
00:13:14.820 --> 00:13:15.960
Comedy recipe.

234
00:13:16.120 --> 00:13:16.480
I know.

235
00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:23.640
Two large eggs, half a cup shortening or vegetable oil, one teaspoon baking powder, half a teaspoon salt, one teaspoon vanilla.

236
00:13:24.320 --> 00:13:24.960
Enjoy.

237
00:13:25.720 --> 00:13:27.660
No nuts, no raisins in that.

238
00:13:27.820 --> 00:13:28.160
Nope.

239
00:13:28.540 --> 00:13:29.820
Just pure banana bread.

240
00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:31.100
Kept it simple.

241
00:13:31.100 --> 00:13:35.620
I hope you're going to post that full recipe in Office Ladies Pod Stories on Instagram.

242
00:13:35.620 --> 00:13:37.800
I will post it exactly as I emailed it to you.

243
00:13:37.860 --> 00:13:38.320
Very good.

244
00:13:39.100 --> 00:13:40.720
Are you still making that banana bread?

245
00:13:40.900 --> 00:13:43.580
